<h1>quadtree</h1>
<p>
(data structure)
</p>

<p>
<strong>Definition:</strong>
A <a href="tree.html"><em>tree</em></a> where each <a href="node.html"><em>node</em></a> is split along all <em>d</em> dimensions, leading to 2<sup>d</sup> <a href="child.html"><em>children</em></a>.
</p>

<p>
<strong>Aggregate parent</strong> (I am a part of or used in ...)<br>
<a href="k2tree.html"><em>k&sup2;-tree</em></a>.
</p>
<p>
<strong>See also</strong>
<a href="octree.html"><em>octree</em></a>, <a href="quadtreecplx.html"><em>quadtree complexity theorem</em></a>, <a href="linearquadtr.html"><em>linear quadtree</em></a>, <a href="bsptree.html"><em>BSP-tree</em></a>.
</p>

<p>
<em>Note:
It was originally designed for 2-dimensional data (pictures), so each node had four children, hence <em>quad</em>tree.   After <a href="../terms.html#GG98">[GG98]</a>.</em>
</p>

<p>Author: <a href="../Other/contrib.html#authorPEB">PEB</a></p>
